WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:01.848
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Augmented reality

00:00:01.848 --> 00:00:04.230
helps people experience digital
information in a new way.

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.230
Join us as we to talk to
Torch about how they're

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:08.200
enabling anyone to
build AR experiences

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.450
on this episode of Stack Chat.

00:00:13.533 --> 00:00:14.700
Thanks for joining us, Josh.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.670
Tell us a little bit more
about what Torch does.

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:18.295
JOSH FAUST: Thanks
for having me, Mark.

00:00:18.295 --> 00:00:21.180
Torch is an AR design
and publishing platform.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.450
So we are a mobile
first workflow

00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:26.030
for building AR inside of AR.

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:31.430
We let designers very
quickly prototype and develop

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:33.350
augmented reality
experiences they can then

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:34.482
share with other people.

00:00:34.482 --> 00:00:36.440
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So what
does the architecture

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:37.857
look like for
people who are going

00:00:37.857 --> 00:00:40.670
to go in and build an AR app?

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:44.840
JOSH FAUST: So we had three main
goals when building Torch 1.0.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.720
We wanted the camera first,
mobile first workflow.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:51.500
We wanted to allow people to
easily bring in whatever assets

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:55.640
they had whether it's images,
video, 3D models, all of that.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:58.040
And then we wanted to
enable collaboration

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:02.790
and semi-realtime collaboration
between users on the platform.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:07.910
So we went with Firebase for the
mobile device portion of that

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:10.910
because it allowed us to
not really pay attention

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:14.720
to a lot of what we needed
to do to offload computation

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.192
into the Cloud.

00:01:16.192 --> 00:01:17.900
Working on a mobile
device, we're already

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:20.750
very limited with CPU and GPU.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:22.640
We're running computer
vision to do AR,

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.830
we're running realtime
3D rendering at 60 frames

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:25.970
a second.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:28.970
So we needed to be able to
shift some of that computation

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:29.972
into the Cloud.

00:01:29.972 --> 00:01:31.430
And Firebase lets
us do that really

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:33.260
easily by calling
Cloud Functions,

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:36.020
by using realtime
databases, things like that.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:38.360
For Asset import,
we wanted to be

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.400
able to support all
sorts of file types

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.590
and to do it in a very
fast and efficient manner.

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:45.530
And so we did that
through a combination

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:47.720
of Firebase Functions and GKE.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.810
So rather than trying to do
any kind of computationally

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:52.890
expensive processing
on the mobile device,

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:54.860
we ship Assets to
the Cloud, run them

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:56.960
through a Cloud
processor in GKE,

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.150
and then ship them
back into Functions

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:01.730
where we then update Firestore.

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:05.990
And that lets the mobile device
know that everything's ready.

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:08.060
Some examples of what
we do in the Cloud

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:11.090
are encoding images
into GPU formats.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:14.820
We do model conversions between
various 3D file formats.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:18.870
We process video for
realtime, things like that.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.860
And then for collaboration,
we are really

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.080
leaning heavily on Firestore.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.120
So we use realtime
snapshotting in order

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:29.630
to let multiple
clients be writing

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:32.930
to the same set of
documents and realtime

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:36.602
update everything that's
happening as it's happening.

00:02:36.602 --> 00:02:38.060
We do this a little
bit differently

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:39.210
than you might think.

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:43.970
So we're not using single
documents as a document store.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:45.350
We're doing time series data.

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:48.200
And what's awesome
is as events sourcing

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:52.190
where every event that has
ever happened in a project,

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:53.900
we know and we track.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:57.230
And then to build
up the final result,

00:02:57.230 --> 00:02:59.750
you run through every event
and generate the full scene

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:02.660
document or the full
project document.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:05.500
And then for performance
and bandwidth reasons,

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:07.910
every once in a while
in a Cloud Function,

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:10.680
we will what we call
checkpoint those events.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:15.260
And so that takes those events
up until a certain time,

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:17.233
turns it into the
final project format.

00:03:17.233 --> 00:03:18.650
And so then to
retrieve a project,

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:20.025
we retrieve the
latest checkpoint

00:03:20.025 --> 00:03:21.567
in any event that
has happened since.

00:03:21.567 --> 00:03:23.275
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
it sounds like a lot

00:03:23.275 --> 00:03:25.140
of the realtime
collaboration, of course,

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:26.382
depends on Firestore.

00:03:26.382 --> 00:03:28.340
What made you choose
Firestore as the right way

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:29.880
to handle this?

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:33.740
JOSH FAUST: So we had two
main reasons for that.

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:35.480
Because we're
primarily a mobile app

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:38.120
and we didn't want to write any
Cloud infrastructure that we

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:41.630
didn't really need to, direct
access to the DBE from mobile

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:46.045
and from Cloud Functions
was really important to us.

00:03:46.045 --> 00:03:47.420
On the other side
of that, that's

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:50.043
a really double edged
sword because it

00:03:50.043 --> 00:03:52.460
means that you need to be
really careful about your schema

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:57.320
management and making sure that
you're not doing anything bad

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:59.780
on either side of those.

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:03.080
And so what we ended up doing
is building a type safe layer

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:06.200
in TypeScript for access to
the database that makes sure

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:10.190
that programmers are
not willy nilly adding

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:14.190
to the schema, changing things
when we don't know about it.

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:18.019
And then we've also added the
ability to version and document

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:21.000
and migrate between versions.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.940
So the second reason
we chose Firestore

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:26.210
is due to that snapshotting that
I talked about earlier where

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:32.390
we can just set up callbacks and
get live data as things change.

00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:34.520
And it's just really easy
to hook into UI, to hook

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.088
into application state.

00:04:36.088 --> 00:04:38.630
And so it just-- it provides us
with a programming model that

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:39.755
makes all that really easy.

00:04:39.755 --> 00:04:41.755
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So it
sounds like you may have

00:04:41.755 --> 00:04:43.410
made some changes over time.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.560
If you could go back
and re-architect this

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:47.540
based on what you
learned over the process,

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:49.320
what would you do differently?

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:52.790
JOSH FAUST: So the
first thing is,

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:57.920
if we were to redo it
today, Cloud Run now exists.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.080
And so a lot of what
we actually built up

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:02.810
in GKE to do Asset
processing we could

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:07.250
do without having to build
what we built in GKE.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:10.520
And so we would probably
move as much of our--

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:13.240
as much of the code that is
in GKE and Cloud Functions

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:14.620
into Cloud Run.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:19.360
But it didn't exist at
the time, so oh, well.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:22.120
The second thing is
schema management.

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:26.680
The schema that we
first chose for Assets

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:29.140
made it very expensive
for us to determine,

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:31.720
does a user have
access to this Asset?

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:35.170
And so what we've ended up with
is, in order to security store

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:38.140
Assets but also
make it very cheap

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:42.880
for users, for clients to
access any information they need

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:48.100
to download in Asset, we do
real time syncing of Asset data

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:49.240
into the project.

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:53.980
So any information that
a client needs in order

00:05:53.980 --> 00:05:58.060
to recreate the project gets
mirrored into the project

00:05:58.060 --> 00:05:59.290
itself.

00:05:59.290 --> 00:05:59.950
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Thanks for telling us

00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:01.825
about how you actually
built this application

00:06:01.825 --> 00:06:04.002
to help other people build
their own applications.

00:06:04.002 --> 00:06:04.960
JOSH FAUST: Absolutely.

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:05.907
Thanks for having me.

00:06:05.907 --> 00:06:07.990
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Get
started with Cloud Firestore

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:11.907
by checking out this
quick start guide.

00:06:11.907 --> 00:06:12.740
Thanks for watching.

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:14.990
And make sure to subscribe
for more great Google Cloud

00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:16.040
Platform content.

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:18.170
We'll see you next
time on Stack Chat.

